// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`methods text > CB-SEM with MLR, FIML and a bootstrap 1`] = `"The model was estimated as a covariance-based structural equation model (CB-SEM) by maximum likelihood with robust (Huber–White) standard errors and a scaled test statistic (MLR; Yuan & Bentler, 2000) with the R package lavaan (version 0.6-19; Rosseel, 2012) in R (version 4.4.2; R Core Team, 2024), through PLS Studio, on N = 412 cases. The model comprises 2 constructs measured by 6 indicators: Anxiety (reflective, 3 indicators) and Depression (reflective, 3 indicators), and 1 structural path (Anxiety → Depression). Each factor was scaled by fixing the loading of its first indicator to 1; standardised estimates (latent and observed variables standardised) are reported alongside the unstandardised ones. Missing values (57 missing cells) were handled by full-information maximum likelihood (FIML; Arbuckle, 1996; Enders & Bandalos, 2001), with robust standard errors and test statistic for incomplete non-normal data (Yuan & Bentler, 2000). Standard errors and 95% bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) confidence intervals came from 2,000 bootstrap resamples (Efron & Tibshirani, 1993), of which 3 failed and were skipped, with two-tailed tests, random seed 99. Global fit was evaluated with the χ² test (scaled), the comparative fit index (CFI), the Tucker–Lewis index (TLI), the root mean square error of approximation (RMSEA) with its 90% confidence interval and the standardised root mean square residual (SRMR), using the robust CFI, TLI and RMSEA (Brosseau-Liard et al., 2012; Brosseau-Liard & Savalei, 2014); following Hu and Bentler (1999), CFI and TLI of at least 0.95, RMSEA of at most 0.06 and SRMR of at most 0.08 indicate good fit, cut-offs that are guidelines rather than tests (Jackson et al., 2009). Convergent validity was assessed by standardised loadings (at least 0.50, ideally 0.70), composite reliability (at least 0.70) and the average variance extracted (at least 0.50; Fornell & Larcker, 1981), and discriminant validity by the heterotrait–monotrait ratio (below 0.85; Henseler et al., 2015)."`;

exports[`methods text > CB-SEM with MLR, FIML and a bootstrap 2`] = `
"% Methods (drafted by PLS Studio; natbib citations, the works in the accompanying .bib file)
The model was estimated as a covariance-based structural equation model (CB-SEM) by maximum likelihood with robust (Huber--White) standard errors and a scaled test statistic \\citep[MLR;][]{yuan2000} with the R package lavaan \\citep[version 0.6-19;][]{rosseel2012} in R \\citep[version 4.4.2;][]{rcore}, through PLS Studio, on N = 412 cases. The model comprises 2 constructs measured by 6 indicators: Anxiety (reflective, 3 indicators) and Depression (reflective, 3 indicators), and 1 structural path (Anxiety $\\rightarrow$ Depression). Each factor was scaled by fixing the loading of its first indicator to 1; standardised estimates (latent and observed variables standardised) are reported alongside the unstandardised ones. Missing values (57 missing cells) were handled by full-information maximum likelihood \\citep[FIML;][]{arbuckle1996,enders2001}, with robust standard errors and test statistic for incomplete non-normal data \\citep{yuan2000}. Standard errors and 95\\% bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) confidence intervals came from 2,000 bootstrap resamples \\citep{efron1993}, of which 3 failed and were skipped, with two-tailed tests, random seed 99. Global fit was evaluated with the $\\chi^2$ test (scaled), the comparative fit index (CFI), the Tucker--Lewis index (TLI), the root mean square error of approximation (RMSEA) with its 90\\% confidence interval and the standardised root mean square residual (SRMR), using the robust CFI, TLI and RMSEA \\citep{brosseau2012,brosseau2014}; following \\citet{hu1999}, CFI and TLI of at least 0.95, RMSEA of at most 0.06 and SRMR of at most 0.08 indicate good fit, cut-offs that are guidelines rather than tests \\citep{jackson2009}. Convergent validity was assessed by standardised loadings (at least 0.50, ideally 0.70), composite reliability (at least 0.70) and the average variance extracted (at least 0.50; \\citealp{fornell1981}), and discriminant validity by the heterotrait--monotrait ratio (below 0.85; \\citealp{henseler2015}).
"
`;

exports[`methods text > PLS-SEM: describes the software, algorithm, model, missing data, bootstrap and criteria 1`] = `"The model was estimated by partial least squares structural equation modelling (PLS-SEM) with the R package cSEM (version 0.7.1; Rademaker & Schuberth, 2020) in R (version 4.5.1; R Core Team, 2025), run in the web browser through webR (version 0.5.4; Stagg & Henry, n.d.), through PLS Studio, on N = 250 cases. The PLS algorithm used the path weighting scheme and stopped when the outer weights changed by less than 10^−7 (at most 300 iterations); it converged after 5 iterations. The indicators were standardised (mean 0, variance 1) before estimation, so loadings, weights and path coefficients are standardised. The model comprises 3 constructs measured by 9 indicators: Image (reflective, 3 indicators), Satisfaction (composite, Mode B, 4 indicators), and Loyalty (reflective, 2 indicators), and 3 structural paths (Image → Satisfaction; Satisfaction → Loyalty; Image → Loyalty). Missing values (12 missing cells) were replaced by the indicator’s mean. Standard errors and 95% percentile confidence intervals came from 5,000 bootstrap resamples (Efron & Tibshirani, 1993), with two-tailed tests, random seed 1234. Following the reporting guidelines of Hair et al. (2019) and Becker et al. (2023), the reflectively measured (Mode A) constructs were assessed by indicator loadings (at least 0.708), internal consistency reliability (Cronbach’s α, ρA and ρC between 0.70 and 0.95; ρA: Dijkstra & Henseler, 2015), convergent validity (average variance extracted, AVE, at least 0.50; Fornell & Larcker, 1981) and discriminant validity (heterotrait–monotrait ratio, HTMT, below 0.85, or 0.90 for conceptually similar constructs; Henseler et al., 2015); the formatively measured (Mode B) constructs by indicator collinearity (VIF below 3; 5 or above is critical) and the significance and relevance of their outer weights. The structural model was assessed by collinearity among predictors (inner VIF below 3), the coefficient of determination R² (0.25, 0.50 and 0.75 as weak, moderate and substantial; Hair et al., 2019), the effect size f² (0.02, 0.15 and 0.35 as small, medium and large; Cohen, 1988) and the significance of the path coefficients by their bootstrap confidence intervals. As a measure of approximate model fit, the SRMR of the estimated model is reported against the threshold of 0.08 (Henseler et al., 2014), with the caveat that fit measures are of limited use in PLS-SEM (Hair et al., 2019)."`;

exports[`methods text > PLS-SEM: describes the software, algorithm, model, missing data, bootstrap and criteria 2`] = `
"% Methods (drafted by PLS Studio; natbib citations, the works in the accompanying .bib file)
The model was estimated by partial least squares structural equation modelling (PLS-SEM) with the R package cSEM \\citep[version 0.7.1;][]{csem} in R \\citep[version 4.5.1;][]{rcore}, run in the web browser through webR \\citep[version 0.5.4;][]{webr}, through PLS Studio, on N = 250 cases. The PLS algorithm used the path weighting scheme and stopped when the outer weights changed by less than $10^{-7}$ (at most 300 iterations); it converged after 5 iterations. The indicators were standardised (mean 0, variance 1) before estimation, so loadings, weights and path coefficients are standardised. The model comprises 3 constructs measured by 9 indicators: Image (reflective, 3 indicators), Satisfaction (composite, Mode B, 4 indicators), and Loyalty (reflective, 2 indicators), and 3 structural paths (Image $\\rightarrow$ Satisfaction; Satisfaction $\\rightarrow$ Loyalty; Image $\\rightarrow$ Loyalty). Missing values (12 missing cells) were replaced by the indicator's mean. Standard errors and 95\\% percentile confidence intervals came from 5,000 bootstrap resamples \\citep{efron1993}, with two-tailed tests, random seed 1234. Following the reporting guidelines of \\citet{hair2019} and \\citet{becker2023}, the reflectively measured (Mode A) constructs were assessed by indicator loadings (at least 0.708), internal consistency reliability (Cronbach's $\\alpha$, $\\rho$A and $\\rho$C between 0.70 and 0.95; $\\rho$A: \\citealp{dijkstra2015}), convergent validity (average variance extracted, AVE, at least 0.50; \\citealp{fornell1981}) and discriminant validity (heterotrait--monotrait ratio, HTMT, below 0.85, or 0.90 for conceptually similar constructs; \\citealp{henseler2015}); the formatively measured (Mode B) constructs by indicator collinearity (VIF below 3; 5 or above is critical) and the significance and relevance of their outer weights. The structural model was assessed by collinearity among predictors (inner VIF below 3), the coefficient of determination R$^2$ (0.25, 0.50 and 0.75 as weak, moderate and substantial; \\citealp{hair2019}), the effect size f$^2$ (0.02, 0.15 and 0.35 as small, medium and large; \\citealp{cohen1988}) and the significance of the path coefficients by their bootstrap confidence intervals. As a measure of approximate model fit, the SRMR of the estimated model is reported against the threshold of 0.08 \\citep{henseler2014}, with the caveat that fit measures are of limited use in PLS-SEM \\citep{hair2019}.
"
`;

exports[`methods text > conditional process analysis (PROCESS model 4, HC3) 1`] = `"The conditional process analysis (model 4 of PROCESS; Hayes, 2022) was estimated with the R package manymome (version 0.2.4; Cheung & Cheung, 2024) in R (version 4.5.1; R Core Team, 2025), run in the web browser through webR (version 0.5.4; Stagg & Henry, n.d.), through PLS Studio, on N = 180 cases. X was Training and Y Performance, with the mediator Efficacy. The variables were Training (observed), Efficacy (mean of 3 items), and Performance (observed). The equations were ordinary least squares regressions, with variables in their original metric (unstandardised coefficients) and HC3 heteroskedasticity-consistent standard errors (Hayes & Cai, 2007; Long & Ervin, 2000). Indirect effects were tested with 95% percentile bootstrap confidence intervals from 10,000 resamples (Efron & Tibshirani, 1993), random seed 2024. The data had no missing values."`;

exports[`methods text > conditional process analysis (PROCESS model 4, HC3) 2`] = `
"% Methods (drafted by PLS Studio; natbib citations, the works in the accompanying .bib file)
The conditional process analysis (model 4 of PROCESS; \\citealp{hayes2022}) was estimated with the R package manymome \\citep[version 0.2.4;][]{cheung2024} in R \\citep[version 4.5.1;][]{rcore}, run in the web browser through webR \\citep[version 0.5.4;][]{webr}, through PLS Studio, on N = 180 cases. X was Training and Y Performance, with the mediator Efficacy. The variables were Training (observed), Efficacy (mean of 3 items), and Performance (observed). The equations were ordinary least squares regressions, with variables in their original metric (unstandardised coefficients) and HC3 heteroskedasticity-consistent standard errors \\citep{hayes2007,long2000}. Indirect effects were tested with 95\\% percentile bootstrap confidence intervals from 10,000 resamples \\citep{efron1993}, random seed 2024. The data had no missing values.
"
`;
